// ============================================================================
// CALL-EDGE-GEN · PROPERTY ACCESS AS A DATA EDGE  (#663)
//
// The TypeScript half of field_access: one row per (property access, resolved member),
// with the direction the data moves and how well the site resolved. `obj.field` had
// nothing sound behind it — every PROPERTY_ACCESS arrives with
// referencedEntityKind = UNKNOWN, 105,051 of them in this repository's own source — so
// "who reads or writes Config.retries" could only be answered by matching a name.
//
// NOTHING NEW IS RESOLVED HERE. expr-type.dl already looks a property access's member up
// on the receiver's type: three rules, one per way a receiver can be typed (a VALUE type,
// a class name's STATIC side, a structural SHAPE), each ending in field_in_scope. Those
// three joins are the resolution; this file pairs them with the access direction and a
// tier. The member lookup carries TypeScript's own shadowing law (a subclass declaration
// hides every inherited one of the same name and staticness), so the row names the
// declaration the access binds to rather than a member somewhere with that name.
//
// AN ACCESSOR IS NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY. `get url()` read as `c.url` is a CALL: the
// runtime invokes the getter, and call-edge-generation/accessors.dl already emits it as
// a call_edges row with kind PROPERTY_READ or PROPERTY_WRITE (#703, #727). Emitting it
// here as well would give one construct two answers in two tables. A consumer asking
// "who reads this member" of an accessor reads call_edges; of a property, field_access.
// The two are disjoint by construction, because field_in_scope holds fields and
// method_in_scope holds accessors.
//
// AN ELEMENT ACCESS IS NOT HERE EITHER. `obj["x"]` with a literal key resolves in
// expr-type.dl, but it is a different node kind and a different site universe, and
// adding it without a measurement would be a guess about how much it is worth. Recorded
// as a gap rather than silently folded in.
//
// TIERS, the same four with the same promises as call_edges:
//   known_edge        exactly one member. There is no dispatch on a property, so this
//                     is the declaration, not an estimate of it.
//   multi_inferred    2+, because the receiver has more than one possible type (a union,
//                     a merged declaration) or two declarations both satisfy the lookup.
//   boundary_lib      the member is declared in a staged library.
//   ambiguous_unknown the receiver could not be typed, or the name is not a member of
//                     what it was typed to. Field = "-", kept as a row.
// ============================================================================

// ── THE SITE UNIVERSE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every PROPERTY_ACCESS that carries a name, MINUS the ones whose member is an accessor.
// property_access_name reads the name off the PROPERTY_NAME child, because a
// PROPERTY_ACCESS row's own literalValue is empty.
//
// The accessor exclusion is what keeps the ambiguous_unknown tier meaning what it says.
// Without it `c.url` on a `get url()` is a field-access site that resolves to no FIELD,
// so it arrives as a declared unknown -- while the engine has in fact resolved it, as a
// call, in call_edges. A tier that reports a blind spot where there is none is worse than
// no row: it makes every consumer's caveat count wrong. The site is excluded and the
// reason is published, as Java publishes its three.
// A METHOD IS NOT A FIELD, in either of the two shapes it takes:
//
//   * `obj.m()`. The parser gives the callee of a call the edge role METHOD_NAME, and it
//     is 343 of immer's 927 PROPERTY_ACCESS nodes -- 37% of the population. Every one of
//     them is already a call_edges row, and as a field-access site each would resolve to
//     no field and arrive as a declared unknown. A tier that reports a blind spot where
//     the engine has in fact resolved the site makes every consumer's caveat count wrong,
//     which is worse than emitting nothing. Excluded on the edge role alone: syntactic,
//     exact, and it needs no member lookup.
//   * `const f = obj.m`, a method read as a VALUE. Not a data edge either, and not a
//     resolved one: which function the value is eventually called through is the shape
//     JavaScript models with callback_registered and TypeScript does not model at all.
//     A known gap, excluded by name rather than left to look like an unresolved field.
ts_field_access_site(e) :- property_access_name(e, _),
    !ts_field_site_is_accessor(e), !ts_field_site_is_method(e).
ts_field_site_is_accessor(e) :- property_access_getter(e, _).
ts_field_site_is_accessor(e) :- property_access_setter(e, _).
ts_field_site_is_method(e) :- expr_kind("client", "PROPERTY_ACCESS", "METHOD_NAME", e).
ts_field_site_is_method(e) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q), expr_type(q, _, qt),
    method_in_scope(qt, n, "false", _).
ts_field_site_is_method(e) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q), expr_static_type(q, _, qt),
    method_in_scope(qt, n, "true", _).
ts_field_site_is_method(e) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q), expr_shape(q, qs),
    method_in_scope(qs, n, _, _).

// ── THE CONSERVATION LEDGER, as Java's copy publishes it ────────────────────
// Every PROPERTY_ACCESS is a site or an exclusion named here, so the difference between
// the IR's population and the relation's is countable rather than assumed.
field_site_excluded(e, "accessor") :- ts_field_site_is_accessor(e).
field_site_excluded(e, "method_callee") :- expr_kind("client", "PROPERTY_ACCESS", "METHOD_NAME", e).
field_site_excluded(e, "method_value") :- ts_field_site_is_method(e),
    !expr_kind("client", "PROPERTY_ACCESS", "METHOD_NAME", e).

// ── RESOLUTION — the three receiver-typing paths expr-type.dl already has ───
ts_field_access_typed(e, f) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q),
    expr_type(q, _, qt),
    field_in_scope(qt, n, "false", f).
ts_field_access_typed(e, f) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q),
    expr_static_type(q, _, qt),
    field_in_scope(qt, n, "true", f).
ts_field_access_typed(e, f) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q),
    expr_shape(q, qs),
    field_in_scope(qs, n, _, f).
ts_field_access_target(e, f) :- ts_field_access_typed(e, f).
ts_field_access_is_typed(e) :- ts_field_access_typed(e, _).

// ── A KEY OF A MODULE-LEVEL CONST TABLE — `TOPICS.orderEvents` ──────────────
// `export const TOPICS = { orderEvents: '…' } as const`: the parser declares each key
// as an OBJECT_LITERAL_PROPERTY owned by the VARIABLE (a nested literal's keys by the
// property holding it). The owner is no type, so none of the three joins above can
// reach it; the receiver names the owner instead — the variable itself, an import of
// it, a namespace import's export of it, or a key of an outer table. Only where the
// typed lookup found nothing: `{ … } satisfies Config` is read as Config's member.
ts_objlit_owner(q, v) :- expr_referenced("client", "VARIABLE", v, q).
ts_objlit_owner(q, v) :- expr_referenced("client", "IMPORT_BINDING", ih, q),
    import_binds(ih, _, "VARIABLE", v).
ts_objlit_owner(q, v) :- property_access_name(q, n),
    expr_child("client", q, "RECEIVER", _, r),
    expr_namespace(r, _, m),
    module_export(_, m, n, "VARIABLE", v).
ts_objlit_owner(q, pf) :- ts_field_access_target(q, pf),
    field_kind("client", "OBJECT_LITERAL_PROPERTY", _, _, pf).
ts_field_access_target(e, f) :- property_access_name(e, n),
    expr_child("client", e, "RECEIVER", _, q),
    ts_objlit_owner(q, o),
    field_decl("client", n, _, o, f),
    field_kind("client", "OBJECT_LITERAL_PROPERTY", _, _, f),
    !ts_field_access_is_typed(e).

// ── ACCESS DIRECTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// property_written and property_write_only are accessors.dl's, and are reused rather
// than restated: the question "is this member at a write position" has one answer, and
// the two relations must not be able to disagree about it.
//   write      a plain assignment; the old value is never read
//   readwrite  a compound assignment or an update (`a.x += 1`, `a.x++`)
//   read       everything else
ts_field_access_kind(e, "write")     :- ts_field_access_site(e), property_written(e), property_write_only(e).
ts_field_access_kind(e, "readwrite") :- ts_field_access_site(e), property_written(e), !property_write_only(e).
ts_field_access_kind(e, "read")      :- ts_field_access_site(e), !property_written(e).

// ── TIER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ts_field_access_count(e, n) :- ts_field_access_site(e),
    n = count : { ts_field_access_target(e, _) }.
ts_field_prov("client", f) :- field_decl("client", _, _, _, f).
ts_field_prov("lib", f) :- field_decl("lib", _, _, _, f).
ts_field_site_leaves_client(e) :- ts_field_access_target(e, f), ts_field_prov("lib", f).
ts_field_access_class(e, "known_edge")        :- ts_field_access_count(e, 1), !ts_field_site_leaves_client(e).
ts_field_access_class(e, "multi_inferred")    :- ts_field_access_count(e, n), n >= 2.
ts_field_access_class(e, "ambiguous_unknown") :- ts_field_access_count(e, 0).

// ── THE CALLER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The enclosing function of the ACCESS, by expr_enclosing_method, exactly as accessors.dl
// attributes an accessor invocation: there is no call site to attribute through. Top-level
// code is the module initializer, which that relation already supplies.
// A read inside a METHOD'S DECORATOR is that method's: `@EventPattern(TOPICS.orderEvents)`
// binds the handler to the topic, and the module initializer that evaluates the
// decorator is no user of it. A decorator's expressions carry no owner hash, so the
// subtree is walked down from the decorator's own expression.
ts_deco_expr_method(e, m) :- decorator_expr("client", e, d), decorator_target("client", _, m, d), m != "".
// ...and so is a read in a PARAMETER'S decorator: `constructor(@inject(TOKENS.Store) s)`
// binds the constructor to the token. The decorator's owner is the parameter.
ts_deco_expr_method(e, m) :- decorator_expr("client", e, d),
    ts_decorator(_, _, "PARAMETER_DECLARATION", p, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, d),
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, m, p), m != "".
ts_deco_expr_method(c, m) :- ts_deco_expr_method(p, m), expr_child("client", p, _, _, c).
ts_field_access_in_decorator(e) :- ts_deco_expr_method(e, _).
// A read in a parameter's DEFAULT is its method's too: `constructor(topic = TOPICS.payments)`
// is evaluated on every call that omits the argument, and the extractor places it in the
// module initializer. Only there: a function written as the default keeps its own reads.
ts_param_default_expr(e, m) :- ts_method_parameter(_, _, m, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e, _, _, _, _),
    e != "", m != "".
ts_param_default_expr(c, m) :- ts_param_default_expr(p, m), expr_child("client", p, _, _, c).
ts_field_access_in_function(e) :- expr_enclosing_method(e, m), m != "", !module_init("client", m, _).
ts_param_default_method(e, m) :- ts_param_default_expr(e, m),
    !ts_field_access_in_function(e), !ts_field_access_in_decorator(e).
ts_field_access_named(e) :- expr_enclosing_method(e, m), m != "".
ts_field_access_placed(e) :- ts_field_access_named(e).
ts_field_access_placed(e) :- ts_field_access_in_decorator(e).
ts_field_access_placed(e) :- ts_param_default_method(e, _).
ts_field_access_from(e, m) :- ts_field_access_site(e), ts_deco_expr_method(e, m).
ts_field_access_from(e, m) :- ts_field_access_site(e), ts_param_default_method(e, m).
ts_field_access_from(e, m) :- ts_field_access_site(e), expr_enclosing_method(e, m), m != "",
    !ts_field_access_in_decorator(e), !ts_param_default_method(e, _).
// ...and the MODULE itself for the residue none of those can place, which is the marker
// call_sites.caller_id already uses in TypeScript rather than a blank. Never NULL, so a
// site can always be attributed to the code that contains it.
ts_field_access_from(e, mod) :- ts_field_access_site(e), !ts_field_access_placed(e),
    expr_module("client", mod, e), mod != "".

// ── THE EXPORTED ROW — field_access(Site, Caller, Field, Prov, Tier, Access) ─
// The same six columns Java emits, so the bundle reads one shape for both front ends.
field_access(e, caller, f, "client", cls, acc) :- ts_field_access_target(e, f),
    ts_field_prov("client", f),
    ts_field_access_from(e, caller), ts_field_access_class(e, cls), ts_field_access_kind(e, acc).
field_access(e, caller, f, "lib", "boundary_lib", acc) :- ts_field_access_target(e, f),
    ts_field_prov("lib", f),
    ts_field_access_from(e, caller), ts_field_access_kind(e, acc).
field_access(e, caller, "-", "-", "ambiguous_unknown", acc) :-
    ts_field_access_class(e, "ambiguous_unknown"),
    ts_field_access_from(e, caller), ts_field_access_kind(e, acc).
// ── AND UNCONDITIONALLY, for any site that emitted no row at all ────────────
// The structural guard Java's copy carries for the same reason: a resolution that is
// removed by a later filter must degrade to a declared unknown, never to silence.
ts_field_access_has_row(e) :- ts_field_access_target(e, f), ts_field_prov(_, f).
field_access(e, caller, "-", "-", "ambiguous_unknown", acc) :- ts_field_access_site(e),
    !ts_field_access_has_row(e),
    ts_field_access_from(e, caller), ts_field_access_kind(e, acc).

// ── A MODULE-SCOPE VARIABLE, READ BY NAME ───────────────────────────────────
// `SERVICE.name`, `f(EnvSchema)`, `TOKENS.IntentStore`: the receiver or argument is an
// IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE that the binder already tied to one declaration -- a module-scope
// variable of its own file (VARIABLE), or an import whose binding import_binds follows,
// through every re-export, to the exported variable (IMPORT_BINDING). Nothing recorded
// that tie as a data edge, so "who reads this exported const" was answered by name, and
// the name matched every same-named const in every sibling package.
//
// The variable is the field of the row: a module is the owner of its variables the way a
// class is the owner of its fields, and consumers read one relation for both. A variable
// that holds a function is a function (`const f = () => ...`): its uses are calls, which
// call_edges already carries, so it is left out here.
ts_module_var(v) :- var_decl("client", _, _, sk, _, _, v), scope_is_module_level(sk),
    !var_is_function(v, _).
ts_var_read_target(e, v) :- expr_kind("client", "IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", _, e),
    expr_referenced("client", "VARIABLE", v, e), ts_module_var(v).
ts_var_read_target(e, v) :- expr_kind("client", "IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", _, e),
    expr_referenced("client", "IMPORT_BINDING", ih, e),
    import_binds(ih, "client", "VARIABLE", v), ts_module_var(v).
// the direction, by the same law property_written applies to a member
ts_var_written(e) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), expr_child("client", asg, "LEFT_OPERAND", _, e),
    expr_kind("client", ak, _, asg), assignment_kind(ak).
ts_var_written(e) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), expr_child("client", u, "UNARY_OPERAND", _, e),
    expr_kind("client", "UNARY_EXPRESSION", _, u), expr_operator("client", op, u), update_operator(op).
ts_var_write_only(e) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), expr_child("client", asg, "LEFT_OPERAND", _, e),
    expr_kind("client", "ASSIGNMENT_EXPRESSION", _, asg).
ts_var_read_kind(e, "write")     :- ts_var_written(e), ts_var_write_only(e).
ts_var_read_kind(e, "readwrite") :- ts_var_written(e), !ts_var_write_only(e).
ts_var_read_kind(e, "read")      :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), !ts_var_written(e).
// the caller, as for a property access: the enclosing function, else the module.
// A DECORATOR on a method or on one of its parameters is that method's: `place(@Payload(new
// Pipe(CommandSchema)) c)` reads CommandSchema for place(). The parser gives an expression
// inside a decorator no owner hash, and expr_enclosing_method routes it to the module
// initializer, so the read is found by walking down from the decorator's own expression.
ts_decorated_method(dx, m) :- decorator_expr("client", dx, d),
    annotation_on("client", _, _, "METHOD_DECLARATION", m, d), m != "".
ts_decorated_method(dx, m) :- decorator_expr("client", dx, d),
    annotation_on("client", _, _, "PARAMETER_DECLARATION", p, d), param_decl("client", _, _, _, m, p), m != "".
ts_under_decorator(dx, dx) :- ts_decorated_method(dx, _).
ts_under_decorator(dx, c) :- ts_under_decorator(dx, p), expr_child("client", p, _, _, c).
ts_var_read_decorated(e, m) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), expr_owner("client", "DECORATOR", _, _, e),
    ts_under_decorator(dx, e), ts_decorated_method(dx, m).
ts_var_read_named(e) :- ts_var_read_decorated(e, _).
ts_var_read_named(e) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), expr_enclosing_method(e, m), m != "".
ts_var_read_from(e, m) :- ts_var_read_decorated(e, m).
ts_var_read_from(e, m) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), !ts_var_read_decorated(e, _),
    expr_enclosing_method(e, m), m != "".
ts_var_read_from(e, mod) :- ts_var_read_target(e, _), !ts_var_read_named(e),
    expr_module("client", mod, e), mod != "".
// one declaration, bound by the binder: known_edge
field_access(e, caller, v, "client", "known_edge", acc) :- ts_var_read_target(e, v),
    ts_var_read_from(e, caller), ts_var_read_kind(e, acc).

// ── THE SAME VARIABLE, READ IN A TYPE: `typeof X` ───────────────────────────
// `type OrderDto = z.infer<typeof OrderSchema>` depends on OrderSchema exactly as
// `f(OrderSchema)` does, but the name sits in a TYPE_QUERY type reference, not in an
// expression, so the binder's expression tie above never sees it. The name is bound
// through the module's own scope (name_binds_any: a declaration of this file, or a named
// import followed to the exported variable). Only the bare form `typeof X`: in
// `typeof A.b` the variable read is A, and the reference names b. A parameter or a local
// of the same name in the enclosing callable shadows the module's (control).
// The site is the type reference; the bundle positions it from the type-reference table.
ts_type_query_site(ref, tn, mod) :- type_ref("client", "TYPE_QUERY", _, tn, ctn, _, _, ref),
    tn != "", ctn = cat("typeof ", tn), type_ref_module("client", mod, ref).
ts_type_query_method(ref, m) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, _, _), type_ref("client", _, _, _, _, m, "METHOD", ref).
ts_type_query_method(ref, m) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, _, _), type_ref("client", _, _, _, _, p, "METHOD_PARAM", ref),
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, m, p).
ts_type_query_method(ref, m) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, _, _), type_ref("client", _, _, _, _, lv, "VARIABLE", ref),
    var_decl("client", _, _, _, m, _, lv), m != "".
ts_type_query_method(ref, m) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, _, _), type_ref("client", _, _, _, _, x, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    expr_enclosing_method(x, m), m != "".
ts_type_query_shadowed(ref) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, tn, _), ts_type_query_method(ref, m),
    param_decl("client", tn, _, _, m, _).
ts_type_query_shadowed(ref) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, tn, _), ts_type_query_method(ref, m),
    var_decl("client", tn, _, _, m, _, _).
ts_type_query_read(ref, v) :- ts_type_query_site(ref, tn, mod), !ts_type_query_shadowed(ref),
    name_binds_any(mod, tn, "client", "VARIABLE", v), ts_module_var(v).
ts_type_query_has_method(ref) :- ts_type_query_method(ref, _).
field_access(ref, m, v, "client", "known_edge", "read") :- ts_type_query_read(ref, v),
    ts_type_query_method(ref, m).
// a type alias, an interface, a class member's annotation: the module's initializer, as for
// any module-level read
field_access(ref, init, v, "client", "known_edge", "read") :- ts_type_query_read(ref, v),
    !ts_type_query_has_method(ref), ts_type_query_site(ref, _, mod), module_init("client", init, mod).
